speechtext:
I propose also to put on Hansard the amendment to the Corning resolution adopted the same day by the Nova Scotia legislature:
Whereas it is the declared and well considered policy of the Liberal party in Nova Scotia, while asserting and upholding constitutional rights and the fullest autonomy for the province, to promote and strengthen the unity of our country;
And, whereas, the Liberal party, as the historic party of progress and reform, fought and won the battle of responsible government, against all the odds of privilege and reaction, and as a party opposed to the last resort the unjust terms of confederation forced upon this province, the crippling effects of which still cause unrest, discontent and a strong sense of grievance among the people;
And, whereas, in the opinion of this House, the final adjustment of those terms must be pressed upon the federal government until full justice is secured and ample redress is made;
And. whereas, it has been and is the policy of the Liberal party in Nova Scotia to advocate and support a Customs tariff which bears lightly and equitably upon the people, for the relief of the burden of taxation and the encouragement of trade and industry ; and to that end it gave its whole-hearted support and advocacy to the reciprocity agreement of 1911, which beneficent measure for the freedom of trade and the relief of taxation, the Conservative party and its leaders opposed and succeeded in defeating ;
And, whereas, that reciprocity agreement, negotiated by a distinguished Nova Scotia statesman and Liberal leader, would have been of immense advantage and benefit to the people of Canada and especially those of this province and would have materially contributed to the amelioration of the difficulties and the removal of the disadvantages imposed upon the industries of Nova Scotia as a constituent part of the Dominion;
And, whereas, this House has already taken active measures to secure concerted Maritime action to the end that discriminations in freight rates, the operation of the Intercolonial Railway in its relation to the commercial and industrial prosperity of Nova Scotia, and the enterprising development of Maritime ports may be speedily adjusted-a consummation there is every reason to anticipate will be accomplished through the new administration at Ottawa which has taken definite steps to restore the constitutional rights of which these provinces were deprived by the former federal government;
Whereas there are growing evidences, under the Liberal administration, of industrial revival and increasing confidence in returning prosperity which is being steadily encouraged and vigorously stimulated by the efficieijt methods and sympathetic attitude of the federal government toward this section of the Dominion, in its endeavour to administer in an enterprising and successful way the tremendous railway undertaking which it inherited from its predecessor in office;
Therefore resolved that this House, recognizing that the factors which have militated against the complete development of this province, have been and are beyond the control of this legislature, and are largely the outcome and the results of action taken at confederation and of policies pursued thereafter by the Conservative party and its leaders, which the Liberal party opposed and resisted through its representatives and by all constitutional measures, place on record its strong and unshaken faith in the future of Nova Scotia and its confidence in the good will and the earnest desire of the present federal government to co-operate, in the most generous spirit, in safeguard-[DOT] mg the rights and promoting the welfare of the people of this province;
Further resolved that this House record its firm determination to maintain, as its paramount duty, the historic policy of the Liberal party and of the government, of loyalty to the best interests of Nova Scotia;
And further resolved that this House, while recognizing the importance and value of the measures already taken by the present parliament of Canada to deal with the problems mentioned in this resolution, reaffirm its determination to exert its full powers and influence in expediting the necessary action to improve and cheapen the means of interprovincial transportation. to enlarge the commercial opportunities of the people, and to develop and expand the foreign trade of Canada through Canadian sea ports; and to the end that Nova Scotia may profit in fullest measure from the advantages of its geographical position and natural resources, urge the renewal of the efforts to secure by negotiation a reciprocal trade agreement with the United States, and freer access to the markets of the world for the products of the Dominion.
Then in 1925, one year ago, the provincial legislature of Nova Scotia passed a resolution from which this is one quotation:
Resolved that in the opinion of this House the Governor in Council should proceed with the least possible delay to prepare or cause to be prepared a statement of the particulars in which the interests of Nova Scotia have been prejudiced by the economic system of the Dominion of Canada, and should make application to the government and parliament of Canada for appropriate redress and equitable remedies.
A commission was appointed in July last by the then provincial government which was not .allowed to function by the new government. The present Premier of Nova Scotia, in an .Address on the Speech from the Throne, announced that they proposed to submit to the legislature a resolution setting out the grievances of that province. Hon. members are pot all at one on this subject. Take Mr. ,McCurdy, Minister of Public Works in the government of my right hon. friend-
